nologin -- System Administration

Lock out logins -- NOT IMPLEMENTED ON THIS SYSTEM
/etc/nologin

/etc/nologin DOES NOTHING HERE.  On other COHERENT releases login(1) looks for
this file and, if it exists, refuses the login and prints the file's contents
instead.  The login(1) this system ships does not read it (base/cmd/login), and
neither does getty(1), so creating /etc/nologin locks nobody out.  Nor is
/etc/trustme, the exemption list that goes with it, read by anything.

This matters because the failure is silent and looks like success: an
administrator who creates the file to close the machine before a backup or a
shutdown gets no error, no warning, and no lockout.  /etc/shutdown therefore
does not create the file either -- see shutdown, "Notes".

To keep users off the machine, take it to single-user mode:

    cd /
    /etc/shutdown single 5

Notes
This page REPLACES the COHERENT 4.2 Lexicon article of the same name, which
describes the feature as working.  The article was shipped on this system for a
facility it does not have; the honest page is the one that says so.  Nothing of
Mark Williams Company's text remains in what you are reading.

Restoring the facility is small and is not done because nobody has asked for
it: login(1) would need to stat the file before it prompts, cat it to the
terminal and exit, exempt uid 0, and read /etc/trustme -- and /etc/rc would
need to remove the file on the way up, as the article says it does.

